# Git detects binary files by looking for a NUL byte near the start, which works
# for all of these -- but core.autocrlf is on for this repository, so the cost of
# being wrong is a checkout that silently rewrites bytes inside an image. A
# corrupted .icns does not fail to build; it produces a macOS bundle whose icon
# the Finder declines to draw, on a machine none of the developers are using.
# Cheap to state, so state it.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary

# Golden reference captures: raw float32, and the one file in the tree where a
# single rewritten byte changes a number rather than breaking a parse.
*.f32 binary

# Shell scripts keep LF on checkout, autocrlf notwithstanding. A CRLF shebang is
# not a shebang: the kernel -- and Git Bash -- take the trailing \r as part of
# the interpreter's name and report `/usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file`. Only
# `./script.sh` breaks, so this would survive every test that ran it as
# `bash script.sh` and fail the first time someone ran it the obvious way.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Patches likewise, and for a sharper reason: a patch's context lines have to
# match the file it is applied to byte for byte. The overlay ports under ports/
# patch sources unpacked from upstream tarballs, which are LF, so a patch
# checked out with CRLF fails to apply on Windows and only on Windows.
*.patch text eol=lf
